During this project, I gained extensive, practical experience in distributed version control and collaborative software development using Git and GitHub. The initial development phase involved creating a local repository, tracking project assets, making structured commits with informative logs, and establishing a remote connection to host the codebase on GitHub. As the project progressed into a collaborative environment, I actively engaged in team-driven workflows by managing remote branches, sharing code contributions, and integrating feature updates with peers. A key highlight of this learning experience was encountering and resolving real-time merge conflicts, which provided critical insights into Git’s internal mechanisms for tracking line-by-line file changes and branch history. Navigating these conflicts required analyzing overlapping code edits, evaluating competing modifications alongside teammates, and executing precise resolutions to preserve codebase integrity without losing essential progress. Mastering these core concepts—from repository initialization and remote syncing to branch management and conflict resolution—has established a strong technical foundation in modern software engineering practices, highlighting the vital role of version control in team collaboration, continuous integration, and long-term project maintenance.